xnu 0410: scan the compositor's surface out at 120 Hz

The panel is a command-mode 120 Hz AMOLED and the compositor's surface is
already 1440x3200 RGBA with the panel's stride, so the copy into the low
carve-out is pure overhead: 2.6 ms of every frame, and it is what a 117 fps
scroll spends its margin on.

`mfs=N` stages the feature: 1 builds the SMMU tables, 2 configures context
bank 3 with translation off, 3 turns it on, 4 also flips SRC0 at the panel's
tick, 5 presents from swap() instead of the tick. The bank identity-maps the
low 4 GiB and places the two compositor surfaces in a 32-bit IOVA window so
SRC0 can carry an IOVA. The tick arms on a fixed deadline chain, takes the
queued record and flips; the renderer waits for that flip. Returning before
it (mfs=6) measured 89-96 fps instead: the GPU is then handed a surface the
display is about to take, and its fence lands inside the tick.

The cache clean moves to the paths that read the surface with the CPU -- the
scaled canvas and the copy. Direct scanout never reads it, and the clean's
0.5 ms sat inside the renderer's wait.

The per-second report is one line instead of a dozen: the old burst held the
work loop past a vsync and the deadline chain skipped a tick, which is a
held frame on the glass. `mondrianfb_verbose=1` restores the detail.

`mondrian_fb_shutdown()` is the reset side of the same work: it puts SRC0
back on the physical carve-out and clears the bank's SCTLR, which is the
state the bootloader expects (0411 calls it).

